        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.752,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.085,
            0.515,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": "Will this work with bigger numbers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.111,
            0.642,
            0.131
          ],
          "content": "You can see that this is true for any place value:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.13,
            0.237,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "1 = 0 + 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.148,
            0.248,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": "1 0 = 9 + 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.166,
            0.271,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": "100 = 99 + 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.185,
            0.294,
            0.203
          ],
          "content": "1000=999+1",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.203,
            0.848,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "10000 = 9999 + 1, and so on. Each digit thus denotes the remainder when the corresponding place value is divided by 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.24,
            0.849,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "For example, to find the remainder of 7309 when divided by 9, we can just add all the digits $7+3+0+9-10$ get 19. This can be seen as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.308,
            0.238,
            0.325
          ],
          "content": "7 × 1000"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.289,
            0.309,
            0.348,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "3 × 100"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.31,
            0.443,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "9 × 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.529,
            0.31,
            0.586,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "7 × 999"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.627,
            0.31,
            0.676,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "3 × 99"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.307,
            0.896,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.571,
            0.486,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "7 \\times 1000 + 3 \\times 100 + 0 \\times 10 + 9 \\times 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.589,
            0.633,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": "7 \\times (999 + 1) + 3 \\times (99 + 1) + 0 \\times (9 + 1) + 9 \\times (0 + 1)",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.608,
            0.747,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": "( 7 \\times 999 + 3 \\times 99 + 0 \\times 9 + 9 \\times 0 ) \\div ( 7 \\times 1 + 3 \\times 1 + 0 \\times 1 + 9 \\times 1 )",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.626,
            0.62,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}= \\underbrace{(7 \\times 999 + 3 \\times 99 + 0 \\times 9 + 9 \\times 0) + (7 + 3 + 0 + 9)}_{}.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.655,
            0.345,
            0.672
          ],
          "content": "This is a multiple of 9"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.474,
            0.656,
            0.748,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": "So, we need to just consider this part"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.68,
            0.848,
            0.774
          ],
          "content": "This means that the number 7309 is 19 more than some multiple of 9. The digits 1 and 9 can further be added to get 1 + 9 = 10. Now, we can say that 7309 is 10 more than a multiple of 9. And repeating this step for the number 10, we get the remainder to be 1 + 0 = 1, meaning 7309 is 1 more than a multiple of 9. Therefore, 7309 ÷ 9 gives a remainder of 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.773,
            0.849,
            0.848
          ],
          "content": "A number is divisible by 9 if and only if the sum of its digits is divisible by 9. Also, we can add the digits of a number repeatedly till a single digit is obtained. This single digit is the remainder when the number is divided by 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.858,
            0.834,
            0.88
          ],
          "content": "？ Look at each of the following statements. Which are correct and why?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.881,
            0.848,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": "(i) If a number is divisible by 9, then the sum of its digits is divisible by 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.872,
            0.915,
            0.92,
            0.937
          ],
          "content": "125"
        }
      ]
    }
